const e="mod-scottish-gaelic-festival",d=2026,n="Read from the English Rules (6 pages), Junior Syllabus (8), Senior Syllabus (5), Virtual Syllabus (1), Guidelines for Families, Schools and Tutors (2), Prescribed Pieces Amendments (3) and the entry site pages (Syllabus, Contact, Products). Placed: dates, entry and own-choice deadlines, how to enter, fluency and age rules, eligibility, membership, all general, choral, duet, instrumental, piping, Cogadh nan Comhlan, media, adjudication, prize-winner, trophy and Gaelic Assessment rules, medal list, and the competition numbers, ages, pieces, time limits and information for each template item. Edition facts are Variables. The documents do NOT print: entry fees (the online system shows them), competition venues or a street address, any marking scheme or points, a time limit for most singing and instrumental classes, the gender limits for the Junior Clarsach and piping, and a contact person for the Mod office other than the Stornoway head office. Not carried by this template and kept as short lines under other: Writers Award and other Senior Literature and Media classes, Virtual Mod classes, Piano. Senior Piping prescribed details are printed only as 'competitors must send 4 copies of each tune'. Differences inside the documents, kept as printed: the TikTok hashtag (#modglaschu2026 in the Junior Syllabus, #modghlaschu2026 in the Senior Syllabus); the Junior Radio class asks for an MP3 file while the rule for Radio and Film says MP4; the Senior Clarsach prescribed pieces are printed as TBC. The Gaelic-language Rules file is not copied; it repeats the English rules. The prescribed-piece amendments correct the wording of specific pieces and are summarised as a news item.",o=JSON.parse(`{"Title":"Rules & Criteria","Subtitle":"The Royal National Mod Glasgow 2026. Read these rules before you enter.","NoticeTitle":"Please Read Before Registering","Notice":"The Mod is on {{ModDates}} in Glasgow. All entries must be complete and paid by {{EntryDeadlineTime}} on {{EntryDeadline}} through the online entry system. Own-choice music is also due by {{OwnChoiceDeadline}}. There are no extensions or exceptions.","Notes":["An Comunn Gaidhealach may refuse an entry.","Changes to the rules are posted on www.ancomunn.co.uk and on social media.","Entry system questions: {{HeadOfficeContact}}."],"Sections":[{"Key":"general","Title":"General Rules","Items":["The Royal National Mod is run by An Comunn Gaidhealach. It has Junior and Senior syllabi, a Virtual syllabus and a set of Rules.","In the syllabus, Open means open to Fluent and Learner competitors. Mixed means open to Boys and Girls (Junior) or to Men and Ladies (Senior).","More information and rules about a specific competition are in the syllabus.","No accompanist is allowed unless the competition information names one.","No competitor may sing the same song in more than one competition.","Prescribed pieces may be sung in any key. Choirs may sing prescribed pieces in their own chosen key.","Competitors may not look at music or words during a performance. This includes tutors or parents mouthing the words or showing the speed or loudness with gestures. This does not apply to instrumental competitions, where competitors may look at music.","The version of the melody on prescribed sheet music is sung as written. The note rhythms can follow Gaelic stresses and local dialect variations, where this does not change the intended rhyme scheme.","Competitors appear in the order drawn in the Mod Programme. The Chairperson makes sure a competition is not delayed because someone does not appear, which may mean taking competitors out of turn.","If competitors are held up at another competition or venue, they must tell the Mod office or the Chief Steward at the competition. Every effort is made to hold a competition open, but how long is at the discretion of An Comunn Gaidhealach.","Parents, carers and teachers who enter a child in several competitions should think about how to get to each competition on time. They must keep the Mod office and the stewards informed of any delays. Include mobile numbers for Mod week in the competitor's entry.","Abusive or disrespectful behaviour towards An Comunn Gaidhealach staff, volunteers, adjudicators or stewards is not tolerated.","At its discretion, An Comunn Gaidhealach may change competitions that have too many entries. Competitions with fewer than three entries may be suspended.","Some categories may have the same song, but separate competitions run. An Comunn Gaidhealach may combine competitions because of low numbers.","Permission is needed before photocopying any sheet music whose copyright belongs to An Comunn Gaidhealach. This does not apply if the tutor's pack or choral pack has been bought.","Please be careful about copying music directly from published books without asking permission.","Competitors tell An Comunn Gaidhealach as soon as possible if they withdraw from a competition.","These medals can only be won once: Gold Medals S104 (F) and S104 (M); Traditional Gold Medals S107 (F) and S107 (M); Oban Times Gold Medal S108; Learners Silver Pendants S114 (F) and S114 (M); Glasgow Skye Association Gold Medals BA100 and BA101; James C. MacPhee Memorial Medals (Junior) S15 (G) and S15 (B); An Comunn Gaidhealach Silver Pendant (Junior) S09 (G); Silver Kilt Pin (Junior) S09 (B); Traditional Silver Pendant (Junior) S23 (G); Traditional Kilt Pin (Junior) S23 (B); Provost of Falkirk Medal (Junior) S27.","The Glasgow Skye Association Gold Medal goes to the aggregate winner of BA100 and BA101. The Cardonald Highlanders Association Cup goes to the most promising learner.","Junior choirs may not sing as their own choice any song prescribed in their section (fluent or learner) in the choral section of the Junior Syllabus for the current Mod. Senior choirs may not sing as their own choice any song prescribed in the choral section of the Senior Syllabus for the current Mod.","For the Mod 2026, An Comunn Gaidhealach keeps the existing fluency rules for choirs."]},{"Key":"eligibility","Title":"Eligibility","Items":["Competitors are in Junior or Senior competitions. Junior age groups use school classes P1 to P7, then 11-12 (Secondary), 13-15 and 16-18. Senior competitions are divided into Ladies, Men, Mixed and Open.","In the Junior section, for competitions marked with an age and not a class, competitors must be within the age group on {{AgeDate}}, the first day of October of the year of the Mod.","Children in P7 in the October of the Mod take part in P7 competitions, whatever their age. The 11-12 competitions are for children in secondary school.","Any competitor under 18 is not eligible for Senior competitions. This does not apply to choral competitions. Young people aged 16 to 18 may sing in a Senior choir.","A competitor should not take part in both Junior and Senior competitions in one year, except in choral competitions.","All Senior solo competitors must be members of An Comunn Gaidhealach. Senior Choirs, Groups, Quartets and Duets must be affiliated, or all be individual members. If membership is not renewed, the entries for that year may be void.","If a child is educated through Gaelic Medium Education (GME) and will be in Primary 2 or above at the time of the Mod, the child must enter fluent competitions.","Children in GME in P1 enter learner competitions. Children from P1 from a fluent background (in a GME school or not), where at least one parent or carer speaks Gaelic at home, enter fluent competitions.","Fluent speakers must not be entered in Learner competitions. Learners may take part in fluent competitions in certain cases, for example Duet competitions.","Parents, carers or teachers of a child with additional support needs, or in an exceptional circumstance, who want to enter the child in a competition must contact An Comunn Gaidhealach. They should use their own judgement when entering children in competitions.","Learners' Choirs may have no more than the agreed share of Gaelic Medium pupils. For the current year this is 50%. The share is reviewed every year.","Competitors in the An Comunn Gaidhealach Gold Medal, Traditional Gold Medal and Silver Pendant competitions must have their fluency level checked again every 3 years.","An Comunn Gaidhealach may refuse entry to a competition when no progress is made in the language. Learners are expected to progress every year through the levels. Competitors must have a Bronze Card before entering the Senior Learners' Traditional competition.","Competitors complete their Gaelic Assessment by {{GaelicAssessmentDeadline}}, in person or online, before the entry deadline. Full details are in the Gaelic Assessment section of the An Comunn Gaidhealach website.","For Virtual competitions, both Junior and Senior competitors are welcome to enter."]},{"Key":"registration","Title":"How to Register","Items":["Complete every entry and pay for it by {{EntryDeadlineTime}} on {{EntryDeadline}} through the online entry system at {{EntrySystem}}. Entries that are incomplete, unpaid or sent after this deadline are rejected automatically.","All own-choice music and other supporting material must be uploaded through the online system by {{OwnChoiceDeadline}}. Late material is not accepted. Email entries are not accepted.","There are no extensions or exceptions to these deadlines. The entrant is responsible for sending on time.","The online system confirms receipt of own-choice music. The competitor must check that own-choice pieces have been sent and show as complete, and contact An Comunn Gaidhealach if an entry is not shown as complete.","For every competition that needs own-choice music (including the premier competition finals), upload a copy of the song words in Gaelic, a copy of the sheet music and an English translation of the words. These three documents must be uploaded before the deadline.","Words (Gaelic) and the English translation are uploaded as text, a Microsoft Word document or an OpenDocument text file, each separate from the music score. Music is uploaded as PDF, Microsoft Word document, OpenDocument text, Sibelius file or a scanned image (TIFF, JPEG, BMP and so on), written in the right form for the instrument.","For traditional competitions only, upload one copy of the words of each song. A translation and music are not needed.","All copies of words and music must be easy to read and clearly marked with the competitor's name and competition number.","Minor changes to own-choice pieces are not accepted after {{MinorChangesDeadline}}.","Instrumental competitors do not have to send music in advance. They must hand clear copies of own-choice music to the adjudicator or chairperson on the day.","Enter the Bands competition with the band's technical specification as part of the online entry.","Senior Piping entry is through the Competing Pipers Association ({{PipingContact}}). Highland Dancing entries are made at {{HighlandDancingEntry}}.","A first-time entrant creates an account on the online entry system. Guidance videos on creating an account and entering are on the blog page of the entry site."]},{"Key":"fees","Title":"Fees","Items":["Entries must be paid for with the entry. {{EntryFees}}.","Printed Competition Programme: {{ProgrammeFee}}","If a trophy is returned late, a fee applies: {{TrophyLateFee}}."]},{"Key":"dates","Title":"Dates","Items":["The Royal National Mod is on {{ModDates}}.","Entries and own-choice material are due by {{EntryDeadline}}. Minor changes to own-choice pieces are not accepted after {{MinorChangesDeadline}}.","TikTok videos must be uploaded by {{TikTokDeadline}}, tagging the Royal National Mod and using the hashtag {{TikTokHashtag}}."]},{"Key":"venue","Title":"Venue","Items":["The Mod is held in {{Venue}}.","The documents print no street address for the competition venues."]},{"Key":"levels","Title":"Rounds and Qualification","Items":["Some competitions are qualifier and final rounds: the Gold Medal Qualifier and Final, the Traditional Gold Medal Qualifier and Final, the Silver Pendant Qualifier and Final (and Oran Mor Gold Medal Qualifier), and Cogadh nan Comhlan heats and finals."]},{"Key":"points","Title":"Results","Items":["The documents print no marking scheme or points. The winner of a competition is called a first prizewinner."]},{"Key":"prizes","Title":"Awards and Trophies","Items":["Awards include Gold Medals, Traditional Gold Medals, the Oban Times Gold Medal, Learners Silver Pendants, Glasgow Skye Association Gold Medals, James C. MacPhee Memorial Medals, Silver Pendants, Silver Kilt Pins, Traditional Silver Pendants, Traditional Kilt Pins and the Provost of Falkirk Medal (see General Rules).","Annual trophies are given in each age group over all dances in Junior Highland Dancing, except the Primary section.","Trophies must be returned, cleaned and engraved (at the winner's cost) to the An Comunn Gaidhealach office ({{TrophyOffice}}) by {{TrophyReturnDeadline}}. The winner must bring it in person or send it by courier. Trophies must not be left or dropped anywhere else.","Tell An Comunn Gaidhealach as early as possible about any damage or work needed on a trophy.","First prizewinners of selected competitions may be asked to take part in filming for the BBC. The BBC manages this.","Virtual competitions give no first, second or third place. Entrants get Commended, Highly Commended or Exceptional."]},{"Key":"judging","Title":"Judging","Items":["Adjudicators' decisions are final.","Adjudication sheets are uploaded directly to the competitor's online entry account. They are no longer available to collect during Mod week.","No marks are given in the Clarsach Ensembles class. The adjudicators give an appreciation of each performance and announce the winners.","In Clarsach Composition, the quality of the composition is judged, not the performance."]},{"Key":"conduct","Title":"Conduct","Items":["Competitors in Junior piping must be ready to appear in the order of play in the programme, or as the Stewards direct. If they are not, they may be disqualified."]},{"Key":"other","Title":"Other Parts of the Syllabus","Items":["Junior and Senior Piano classes (I01, I02) and the Junior Literacy, Radio, Film and TikTok classes are not separate items of this template (the Junior ones are described under Art).","Virtual Mod classes AL100 to AL104 (Solo Singing, Solo Traditional, Group Singing, Recitation, Instrumental) and TikTok for Junior and Senior entrants (Open or Mixed, own choice): AL100 one song; AL101 one song in the traditional style; AL102 duets, quartets or choirs in unison or harmony, one song; AL103 reciting a poem or telling a story, up to 5 minutes; AL104 a set of tunes on an instrument of choice (Piano, Fiddle, Accordion, Melodeon, Chanter, Bagpipes or Clarsach), 2 to 5 minutes.","Writers Award (Senior): L100 best book for young children, L101 best book for teenagers and L103 best book for adults, published in the current year; L104 New Song (new Gaelic song put to music, previous winners not eligible for three years); L105 Play; L106 non-fiction prose under 2000 words; L107 Poetry (three poems, previous winners not eligible for three years); L108 short story under 2000 words; L109 Choral Arrangement (for adult male, female or mixed voices, own choice of a song from the Gaelic tradition); L110 Writing - Students (max 2000 words, open to students in a university or other higher education institution); L111 Flash Fiction (short story up to 500 words); L112 Podcast (a single original episode in Gaelic, entrants aged 18 and over)."]},{"Key":"reporting","Title":"Media, Art and Literature Submissions","Items":["All submissions must be the entrant's original, uncorrected work.","Pen-names must be given and marked clearly on each page of written or typed work. Pen-names are used in all literary competitions because they are the names the adjudicators see.","Literature: all work must be typewritten and sent as a PDF.","Art: all work is sent as a PDF or JPEG.","Radio and Film: all work is sent as an MP4 file. (The Junior Radio Piece / Podcast class asks for an MP3 file.)","All submissions must reach the online entry system by {{EntryDeadline}}."]}],"Documents":[{"Title":"Royal National Mod 2026 Syllabus page (all files)","Url":"https://enter.ancomunn.co.uk/pages/syllabus"},{"Title":"Rules of The Royal National Mod (English)","Url":"https://enter.ancomunn.co.uk/file/pdf/pdf/Rules_English_acgmod_event_18"},{"Title":"Junior Syllabus 2026","Url":"https://enter.ancomunn.co.uk/file/pdf/pdf/Syllabus_Junior_acgmod_event_18"},{"Title":"Senior Syllabus 2026","Url":"https://enter.ancomunn.co.uk/file/pdf/pdf/Syllabus_Senior_acgmod_event_18"},{"Title":"Virtual Syllabus 2026","Url":"https://enter.ancomunn.co.uk/file/pdf/pdf/Syllabus_Virtual_acgmod_event_18"},{"Title":"Guidelines for Families, Schools and Tutors","Url":"https://enter.ancomunn.co.uk/file/pdf/pdf/Guidelines_acgmod_event_18"},{"Title":"Prescribed Pieces Amendments","Url":"https://enter.ancomunn.co.uk/file/pdf/pdf/Prescribed_Changes_acgmod_event_18"}]}`),i=[{ParticipantType:"Team",Source:"Rules (English, Jan 2026) Duets, Quartets and Harmony Singing; Choral; Cogadh nan Comhlan",Sections:{About:null,TimeAndFormat:null,Rules:["A competitor must not sing in more than one duet or group in the same competition.","A competitor may not play in more than one group in the same competition.","Junior choirs have at least 8 and at most 30 voices. Senior mixed choirs, area and rural choirs, and ladies and men choirs have at least 10 and at most 35 voices.","A competitor may not sing in more than one choir in the same competition.","Conductors must not sing with their choirs. Choir members and conductors may not look at words or music while competing."],Allowed:null,NotAllowed:null,Judging:null,Points:null,Notes:null},Markdown:`## Rules
- A competitor must not sing in more than one duet or group in the same competition.
- A competitor may not play in more than one group in the same competition.
- Junior choirs have at least 8 and at most 30 voices. Senior mixed choirs, area and rural choirs, and ladies and men choirs have at least 10 and at most 35 voices.
- A competitor may not sing in more than one choir in the same competition.
- Conductors must not sing with their choirs. Choir members and conductors may not look at words or music while competing.`}],a=[{AgeGroup:"P1-P3",Source:"Guidelines for Families, Schools and Tutors; Rules (English, Jan 2026) Fluency",Sections:{About:null,TimeAndFormat:null,Rules:["These are school classes. Your class is the class at the time of the Mod.","P1 fluent: only children with Gaelic spoken at home by one or more parents or carers. Preparation starts during the last stages of nursery and the five weeks in P1 before the Mod.","P1 learner: for learners at an early stage of Gaelic, in any school including P1 GME pupils with no Gaelic at home. After the Mod, children in GME move to the P2 fluent competition.","Preparation for the P2 and P3 competitions starts in January of the class before.","All learner competitions from P2 to P7 are mixed competitions."],Allowed:null,NotAllowed:null,Judging:null,Points:null,Notes:null},Markdown:`## Rules
- These are school classes. Your class is the class at the time of the Mod.
- P1 fluent: only children with Gaelic spoken at home by one or more parents or carers. Preparation starts during the last stages of nursery and the five weeks in P1 before the Mod.
- P1 learner: for learners at an early stage of Gaelic, in any school including P1 GME pupils with no Gaelic at home. After the Mod, children in GME move to the P2 fluent competition.
- Preparation for the P2 and P3 competitions starts in January of the class before.
- All learner competitions from P2 to P7 are mixed competitions.`},{AgeGroup:"P4-P5",Source:"Guidelines for Families, Schools and Tutors",Sections:{About:null,TimeAndFormat:null,Rules:["These are school classes at the time of the Mod. Preparation starts in January of the class before.","All learner competitions from P2 to P7 are mixed competitions."],Allowed:null,NotAllowed:null,Judging:null,Points:null,Notes:null},Markdown:`## Rules
- These are school classes at the time of the Mod. Preparation starts in January of the class before.
- All learner competitions from P2 to P7 are mixed competitions.`},{AgeGroup:"P6-P7",Source:"Guidelines for Families, Schools and Tutors",Sections:{About:null,TimeAndFormat:null,Rules:["These are school classes at the time of the Mod. Preparation starts in January of the class before. Children in P7 in the October of the Mod take part in P7 competitions, whatever their age.","Preparation for the 11-12 (Secondary) competition starts in January of P7."],Allowed:null,NotAllowed:null,Judging:null,Points:null,Notes:null},Markdown:`## Rules
- These are school classes at the time of the Mod. Preparation starts in January of the class before. Children in P7 in the October of the Mod take part in P7 competitions, whatever their age.
- Preparation for the 11-12 (Secondary) competition starts in January of P7.`},{AgeGroup:"11-12 (Sec.)",Source:"Guidelines for Families, Schools and Tutors",Sections:{About:null,TimeAndFormat:null,Rules:["The 11-12 competitions are for children in secondary school."],Allowed:null,NotAllowed:null,Judging:null,Points:null,Notes:null},Markdown:`## Rules
- The 11-12 competitions are for children in secondary school.`},{AgeGroup:"13-15 Years",Source:"Rules (English, Jan 2026) Age",Sections:{About:null,TimeAndFormat:null,Rules:["Competitors must be 13 to 15 years old on {{AgeDate}}."],Allowed:null,NotAllowed:null,Judging:null,Points:null,Notes:null},Markdown:`## Rules
- Competitors must be 13 to 15 years old on {{AgeDate}}.`},{AgeGroup:"16-18 Years",Source:"Rules (English, Jan 2026) Age; Choral rule 73",Sections:{About:null,TimeAndFormat:null,Rules:["Competitors must be 16 to 18 years old on {{AgeDate}}.","Young people between 16 and 18 may sing in a Senior choir."],Allowed:null,NotAllowed:null,Judging:null,Points:null,Notes:null},Markdown:`## Rules
- Competitors must be 16 to 18 years old on {{AgeDate}}.
- Young people between 16 and 18 may sing in a Senior choir.`},{AgeGroup:"Senior - Ladies",Source:"Senior Syllabus p.1 Key",Sections:{About:null,TimeAndFormat:null,Rules:["Ladies' competitions, for female competitors aged 18 and over (Senior)."],Allowed:null,NotAllowed:null,Judging:null,Points:null,Notes:null},Markdown:`## Rules
- Ladies' competitions, for female competitors aged 18 and over (Senior).`},{AgeGroup:"Senior - Men",Source:"Senior Syllabus p.1 Key",Sections:{About:null,TimeAndFormat:null,Rules:["Men's competitions, for male competitors aged 18 and over (Senior)."],Allowed:null,NotAllowed:null,Judging:null,Points:null,Notes:null},Markdown:`## Rules
- Men's competitions, for male competitors aged 18 and over (Senior).`},{AgeGroup:"Senior - Mixed",Source:"Senior Syllabus p.1 Key",Sections:{About:null,TimeAndFormat:null,Rules:["Mixed competitions are open to Men and Ladies."],Allowed:null,NotAllowed:null,Judging:null,Points:null,Notes:null},Markdown:`## Rules
- Mixed competitions are open to Men and Ladies.`},{AgeGroup:"Senior - Open",Source:"Senior Syllabus p.1 Key",Sections:{About:null,TimeAndFormat:null,Rules:["Open competitions are open to Fluent and Learner competitors."],Allowed:null,NotAllowed:null,Judging:null,Points:null,Notes:null},Markdown:`## Rules
- Open competitions are open to Fluent and Learner competitors.`}],t=JSON.parse(`[{"Title":"Solo Singing","Source":"Junior Syllabus p.1-2; Senior Syllabus p.1-3","Sections":{"About":["Junior Fluent solos S01 to S09 (P1 to 13-15), each a prescribed song, with girls and boys separate from P2.","Junior Learner solos S10 to S15 (P1 to 13-15), each a prescribed song. S15 is the James C. MacPhee Medal.","Junior Open solos: S16 (16-18, own choice, one song) and S28 (Under 19 Mixed, open to previous winners of other competitions, own choice).","Senior Fluent Gold Medal: S100 Ladies and S101 Men qualifiers (one own choice song), S102 Ladies and S103 Men Oran Mor qualifiers (a prescribed song), S104 (F) and S104 (M) Gold Medal Finals (two songs, one prescribed and one own choice).","S108 Oban Times Gold Medal: a prescribed song composed by a Lorn bard.","Senior Learners Silver Pendant: S110 Ladies and S111 Men qualifiers (a prescribed song), S112 Ladies and S113 Men qualifiers (one own choice song on the theme {{SeniorSolosTheme}}), S114 (F) and S114 (M) Finals (two songs, one prescribed and one own choice).","Senior Learners and Open: S115 Solo CalMac competition (own choice song from any area of Scotland), S118 Mull and Iona (a prescribed song), S119 Skye and Sutherland (a song relating to Skye or Sutherland), S121 Solo Singing with Instrument (self-accompaniment, or an accompanist of your own choice, one Gaelic song with any instrument except Clarsach)."],"TimeAndFormat":null,"Rules":["The singing is in Scottish Gaelic.","Own-choice music must be uploaded by {{OwnChoiceDeadline}} (words in Gaelic, music and an English translation).","The prescribed-piece amendments uploaded 11/05/26 change S04, S11, S15, S103 and S114 (M). Use the corrected version."],"Allowed":null,"NotAllowed":null,"Judging":null,"Points":null,"Notes":null},"Markdown":"## About this competition\\n- Junior Fluent solos S01 to S09 (P1 to 13-15), each a prescribed song, with girls and boys separate from P2.\\n- Junior Learner solos S10 to S15 (P1 to 13-15), each a prescribed song. S15 is the James C. MacPhee Medal.\\n- Junior Open solos: S16 (16-18, own choice, one song) and S28 (Under 19 Mixed, open to previous winners of other competitions, own choice).\\n- Senior Fluent Gold Medal: S100 Ladies and S101 Men qualifiers (one own choice song), S102 Ladies and S103 Men Oran Mor qualifiers (a prescribed song), S104 (F) and S104 (M) Gold Medal Finals (two songs, one prescribed and one own choice).\\n- S108 Oban Times Gold Medal: a prescribed song composed by a Lorn bard.\\n- Senior Learners Silver Pendant: S110 Ladies and S111 Men qualifiers (a prescribed song), S112 Ladies and S113 Men qualifiers (one own choice song on the theme {{SeniorSolosTheme}}), S114 (F) and S114 (M) Finals (two songs, one prescribed and one own choice).\\n- Senior Learners and Open: S115 Solo CalMac competition (own choice song from any area of Scotland), S118 Mull and Iona (a prescribed song), S119 Skye and Sutherland (a song relating to Skye or Sutherland), S121 Solo Singing with Instrument (self-accompaniment, or an accompanist of your own choice, one Gaelic song with any instrument except Clarsach).\\n\\n## Rules\\n- The singing is in Scottish Gaelic.\\n- Own-choice music must be uploaded by {{OwnChoiceDeadline}} (words in Gaelic, music and an English translation).\\n- The prescribed-piece amendments uploaded 11/05/26 change S04, S11, S15, S103 and S114 (M). Use the corrected version."},{"Title":"Traditional Singing","Source":"Junior Syllabus p.2; Senior Syllabus p.1-2","Sections":{"About":["Junior Fluent Traditional: S20 Under 11 Girls, S21 11-12 Girls, S22 Under 13 Boys, S23 (B) and S23 (G) 13-15, S24 (B) and S24 (G) 16-18. One song sung in the traditional style, own choice.","Junior Learner Traditional: S25 Under 13 Mixed, S26 13-15 Mixed, S27 16-18 Mixed (Provost of Falkirk Medal). One song in the traditional style.","Senior Fluent Traditional Gold Medal: S105 Ladies and S106 Men qualifiers (one song in the traditional style, own choice), S107 (F) and S107 (M) Finals (two songs in the traditional style, own choice).","S116 Senior Learners' Traditional Singing: a song in the traditional style."],"TimeAndFormat":null,"Rules":["For traditional competitions only, upload one copy of the words of each song. A translation and music are not needed."],"Allowed":null,"NotAllowed":null,"Judging":null,"Points":null,"Notes":null},"Markdown":"## About this competition\\n- Junior Fluent Traditional: S20 Under 11 Girls, S21 11-12 Girls, S22 Under 13 Boys, S23 (B) and S23 (G) 13-15, S24 (B) and S24 (G) 16-18. One song sung in the traditional style, own choice.\\n- Junior Learner Traditional: S25 Under 13 Mixed, S26 13-15 Mixed, S27 16-18 Mixed (Provost of Falkirk Medal). One song in the traditional style.\\n- Senior Fluent Traditional Gold Medal: S105 Ladies and S106 Men qualifiers (one song in the traditional style, own choice), S107 (F) and S107 (M) Finals (two songs in the traditional style, own choice).\\n- S116 Senior Learners' Traditional Singing: a song in the traditional style.\\n\\n## Rules\\n- For traditional competitions only, upload one copy of the words of each song. A translation and music are not needed."},{"Title":"Puirt-a-Beul","Source":"Senior Syllabus p.2","Sections":{"About":["S117 Senior Open Puirt-a-beul: singing a puirt-a-beul, own choice.","Puirt-a-beul for choirs is in the Choral Singing classes (Junior C03, C06, C10, C13 and Senior C102, C106)."],"TimeAndFormat":null,"Rules":["Own-choice music must be uploaded by {{OwnChoiceDeadline}} (words in Gaelic, music and an English translation)."],"Allowed":null,"NotAllowed":null,"Judging":null,"Points":null,"Notes":null},"Markdown":"## About this competition\\n- S117 Senior Open Puirt-a-beul: singing a puirt-a-beul, own choice.\\n- Puirt-a-beul for choirs is in the Choral Singing classes (Junior C03, C06, C10, C13 and Senior C102, C106).\\n\\n## Rules\\n- Own-choice music must be uploaded by {{OwnChoiceDeadline}} (words in Gaelic, music and an English translation)."},{"Title":"Waulking Song","Source":"Junior Syllabus p.3; Senior Syllabus p.3","Sections":{"About":["Junior C15 Under 19 (Open) and Senior C113 (Mixed)."],"TimeAndFormat":["Time limit: 10 minutes."],"Rules":["Sing waulking songs of your own choice. Any number of songs may be sung. They may, but need not, include an oran basaidh.","Groups must have at least 7 people, with one or more soloists if wanted.","The singing is in unison. Any change from unison singing must follow local traditional ways of singing waulking songs.","Spoken words as part of the performance, or before another song, are the group's choice."],"Allowed":null,"NotAllowed":null,"Judging":null,"Points":null,"Notes":null},"Markdown":"## About this competition\\n- Junior C15 Under 19 (Open) and Senior C113 (Mixed).\\n\\n## Time and format\\n- Time limit: 10 minutes.\\n\\n## Rules\\n- Sing waulking songs of your own choice. Any number of songs may be sung. They may, but need not, include an oran basaidh.\\n- Groups must have at least 7 people, with one or more soloists if wanted.\\n- The singing is in unison. Any change from unison singing must follow local traditional ways of singing waulking songs.\\n- Spoken words as part of the performance, or before another song, are the group's choice."},{"Title":"Action Song","Source":"Junior Syllabus p.3","Sections":{"About":["Junior C14 Under 13 (Open), own choice."],"TimeAndFormat":null,"Rules":["A song with actions that show the theme, or a demonstration of labour action with song."],"Allowed":null,"NotAllowed":null,"Judging":null,"Points":null,"Notes":null},"Markdown":"## About this competition\\n- Junior C14 Under 13 (Open), own choice.\\n\\n## Rules\\n- A song with actions that show the theme, or a demonstration of labour action with song."},{"Title":"Precenting a Psalm","Source":"Junior Syllabus p.3; Senior Syllabus p.2","Sections":{"About":["Junior C16 Under 13 (Open), C17 Under 19 (Open); Senior S120 (Open)."],"TimeAndFormat":null,"Rules":["Precent, in a traditional way, 2 verses of an own-choice psalm to a tune chosen from the Scottish Psalmody.","The competitor's own choir or group gives the congregation support.","The group must have at least 6 people."],"Allowed":null,"NotAllowed":null,"Judging":null,"Points":null,"Notes":null},"Markdown":"## About this competition\\n- Junior C16 Under 13 (Open), C17 Under 19 (Open); Senior S120 (Open).\\n\\n## Rules\\n- Precent, in a traditional way, 2 verses of an own-choice psalm to a tune chosen from the Scottish Psalmody.\\n- The competitor's own choir or group gives the congregation support.\\n- The group must have at least 6 people."},{"Title":"Duet Singing","Source":"Junior Syllabus p.3; Senior Syllabus p.2","Sections":{"About":["Junior C18 Under 13 and C19 13-18 (Duet - Mixed - Open); Senior C110 Duet Singing (Mixed)."],"TimeAndFormat":null,"Rules":["One song, own choice, in harmony.","Own-choice music must be uploaded by {{OwnChoiceDeadline}} (words in Gaelic, music and an English translation).","A learner may take part in a fluent duet competition."],"Allowed":null,"NotAllowed":null,"Judging":null,"Points":null,"Notes":null},"Markdown":"## About this competition\\n- Junior C18 Under 13 and C19 13-18 (Duet - Mixed - Open); Senior C110 Duet Singing (Mixed).\\n\\n## Rules\\n- One song, own choice, in harmony.\\n- Own-choice music must be uploaded by {{OwnChoiceDeadline}} (words in Gaelic, music and an English translation).\\n- A learner may take part in a fluent duet competition."},{"Title":"Choral Singing","Source":"Junior Syllabus p.2-3; Senior Syllabus p.2-3","Sections":{"About":["Junior Choral Fluent C01 (Unison), C02 (Harmony), C03 (Puirt-a-Beul), Under 13. Junior Choral Learner C04, C05, C06, Under 13 (area, district or town choir).","Junior Choral Open: C07 Under 13 Unison (primary schools or community groups in rural areas, excluding towns with 2000 people or more; more than one school or group may form one choir), C08 and C09 13-18, C10 13-18 Puirt-a-beul, C11, C12 and C13 Under 19 (area, district or town choir).","Senior Choral: C100 Rural Choirs Lorn Shield, C101 Rural Choirs Sheriff MacMaster, C102 Rural Puirt-a-beul, C103 Rural Ladies, C104 Area Lovat and Tullibardine, C105 Area Margrat Duncan, C106 Area Puirt-a-beul, C107 Area Ladies, C108 Open Men, C109 Open Accompanied Choir (own accompanist, one song accompanied by piano), C111 Quartet Singing, C112 Harmony Singing (groups of 5 to 10 singers, one song in harmony, own choice).","Most Junior and Senior choral classes: one prescribed song and one own choice. The puirt-a-beul classes: prescribed puirt-a-beul only."],"TimeAndFormat":null,"Rules":["Rural choirs are made up from non-urban areas.","In Junior class C07, choirs are from primary schools or community groups in areas with a population of 2000 or less.","Bar numbering and words for C102, C103, C106, C107 and C108 were corrected in the 11/05/26 amendments, and for C01, C03, C04 and C13.","Own-choice music must be uploaded by {{OwnChoiceDeadline}} (words in Gaelic, music and an English translation)."],"Allowed":null,"NotAllowed":null,"Judging":null,"Points":null,"Notes":null},"Markdown":"## About this competition\\n- Junior Choral Fluent C01 (Unison), C02 (Harmony), C03 (Puirt-a-Beul), Under 13. Junior Choral Learner C04, C05, C06, Under 13 (area, district or town choir).\\n- Junior Choral Open: C07 Under 13 Unison (primary schools or community groups in rural areas, excluding towns with 2000 people or more; more than one school or group may form one choir), C08 and C09 13-18, C10 13-18 Puirt-a-beul, C11, C12 and C13 Under 19 (area, district or town choir).\\n- Senior Choral: C100 Rural Choirs Lorn Shield, C101 Rural Choirs Sheriff MacMaster, C102 Rural Puirt-a-beul, C103 Rural Ladies, C104 Area Lovat and Tullibardine, C105 Area Margrat Duncan, C106 Area Puirt-a-beul, C107 Area Ladies, C108 Open Men, C109 Open Accompanied Choir (own accompanist, one song accompanied by piano), C111 Quartet Singing, C112 Harmony Singing (groups of 5 to 10 singers, one song in harmony, own choice).\\n- Most Junior and Senior choral classes: one prescribed song and one own choice. The puirt-a-beul classes: prescribed puirt-a-beul only.\\n\\n## Rules\\n- Rural choirs are made up from non-urban areas.\\n- In Junior class C07, choirs are from primary schools or community groups in areas with a population of 2000 or less.\\n- Bar numbering and words for C102, C103, C106, C107 and C108 were corrected in the 11/05/26 amendments, and for C01, C03, C04 and C13.\\n- Own-choice music must be uploaded by {{OwnChoiceDeadline}} (words in Gaelic, music and an English translation)."},{"Title":"Storytelling","Source":"Junior Syllabus p.4","Sections":{"About":["Junior Storytelling Open: BA16 5-8 Years, BA17 9-10 Years, BA18 11-12 Years, BA19 13-15 Years, all Mixed. BA20 16-18 Years (Mixed) is Poetry Recitation or Storytelling."],"TimeAndFormat":["Maximum time: 4 minutes (BA16 to BA19). BA20 is 5 minutes."],"Rules":["Tell a story of your own choice."],"Allowed":null,"NotAllowed":null,"Judging":null,"Points":null,"Notes":null},"Markdown":"## About this competition\\n- Junior Storytelling Open: BA16 5-8 Years, BA17 9-10 Years, BA18 11-12 Years, BA19 13-15 Years, all Mixed. BA20 16-18 Years (Mixed) is Poetry Recitation or Storytelling.\\n\\n## Time and format\\n- Maximum time: 4 minutes (BA16 to BA19). BA20 is 5 minutes.\\n\\n## Rules\\n- Tell a story of your own choice."},{"Title":"Poetry","Source":"Junior Syllabus p.4; Senior Syllabus p.3","Sections":{"About":["Junior Poetry Recitation Fluent BA01 to BA09 (P1 to 13-15) and Learner BA10 to BA15 (P1 to 13-15), each a prescribed piece. BA20 16-18 Poetry Recitation or Storytelling (own choice).","Senior Open Recitation: BA100 Poetry and BA101 Passage of Prose, Mixed, own choice, on the theme {{OpenRecitationTheme}}."],"TimeAndFormat":["BA20: maximum time 5 minutes."],"Rules":["Junior prescribed pieces are recited with no actions.","In BA100 and BA101, give an introduction to your chosen piece before reading. The aggregate winner of these two competitions gets the Glasgow Skye Association Gold Medal."],"Allowed":null,"NotAllowed":null,"Judging":null,"Points":null,"Notes":null},"Markdown":"## About this competition\\n- Junior Poetry Recitation Fluent BA01 to BA09 (P1 to 13-15) and Learner BA10 to BA15 (P1 to 13-15), each a prescribed piece. BA20 16-18 Poetry Recitation or Storytelling (own choice).\\n- Senior Open Recitation: BA100 Poetry and BA101 Passage of Prose, Mixed, own choice, on the theme {{OpenRecitationTheme}}.\\n\\n## Time and format\\n- BA20: maximum time 5 minutes.\\n\\n## Rules\\n- Junior prescribed pieces are recited with no actions.\\n- In BA100 and BA101, give an introduction to your chosen piece before reading. The aggregate winner of these two competitions gets the Glasgow Skye Association Gold Medal."},{"Title":"Drama","Source":"Junior Syllabus p.5; Senior Syllabus p.3","Sections":{"About":["Junior D01 Under 13 and D02 Under 19 (Drama - Open); Senior D101 Drama (Open)."],"TimeAndFormat":["Junior: 20 minutes, including set-up and removal of props."],"Rules":["Junior: an original play devised and staged by the school or group presenting the playlet.","Senior: a One Act Play. Teams need at least 3 actors."],"Allowed":null,"NotAllowed":null,"Judging":null,"Points":null,"Notes":null},"Markdown":"## About this competition\\n- Junior D01 Under 13 and D02 Under 19 (Drama - Open); Senior D101 Drama (Open).\\n\\n## Time and format\\n- Junior: 20 minutes, including set-up and removal of props.\\n\\n## Rules\\n- Junior: an original play devised and staged by the school or group presenting the playlet.\\n- Senior: a One Act Play. Teams need at least 3 actors."},{"Title":"Piping","Source":"Rules (English, Jan 2026) Piping Competitions; Junior Syllabus p.6; Senior Syllabus p.4","Sections":{"About":["Junior piping (Chanter and Bagpipes) is P01 to P11. Senior piping is P101 to P104 for Premier, A Grade and B Grade players, Ceol Mor and Ceol Beag."],"TimeAndFormat":null,"Rules":["Senior Piping competitions are run by The Competing Pipers Association ({{PipingContact}}).","Senior competitors must send 4 copies of each tune to the Competing Pipers Association.","Competitors in Junior piping events must be in the order of play in the programme, or as the Stewards direct, or they may be disqualified."],"Allowed":null,"NotAllowed":null,"Judging":null,"Points":null,"Notes":null},"Markdown":"## About this competition\\n- Junior piping (Chanter and Bagpipes) is P01 to P11. Senior piping is P101 to P104 for Premier, A Grade and B Grade players, Ceol Mor and Ceol Beag.\\n\\n## Rules\\n- Senior Piping competitions are run by The Competing Pipers Association ({{PipingContact}}).\\n- Senior competitors must send 4 copies of each tune to the Competing Pipers Association.\\n- Competitors in Junior piping events must be in the order of play in the programme, or as the Stewards direct, or they may be disqualified."},{"Title":"Bagpipes","Source":"Rules (English, Jan 2026) Piping; Junior Syllabus p.6; Senior Syllabus p.4","Sections":{"About":["Junior Bagpipes: P03 Novice (a 2/4 March; for pipers in their first full year of playing), P04 Under 16 (a 2/4 March, at least 4 parts), P05 Under 16 (Strathspey and Reel, at least 4 parts), P06 Under 16 (a Jig, at least 4 parts), P07 Under 16 (the ground of a Piobaireachd), P08 Under 16 (Piobaireachd), P09 16-18 (a 2/4 March, Strathspey and Reel, at least 4 parts each), P10 16-18 (a Hornpipe and Jig), P11 16-18 (Piobaireachd). All own choice.","Senior Bagpipes: P101 Premier and A Grade players, Ceol Mor (Piobaireachd, competitor's choice); P102 Premier and A Grade players, Ceol Beag (a March, Strathspey and Reel); P103 B Grade players, Ceol Mor (Piobaireachd, competitor's choice); P104 B Grade players, Ceol Beag (a March, Strathspey and Reel)."],"TimeAndFormat":null,"Rules":["Junior piping is open to players aged 18 and under who have not competed in any adult Bagpipe competition.","Competitors must be in the age group on {{AgeDate}}.","Competitors in P03 cannot enter other events except P07, subject to age.","Competitors entering P08 cannot also compete in P07.","Competitors who have played a full Piobaireachd in any competition cannot compete in P07.","Competitors may only enter events in their own age group."],"Allowed":null,"NotAllowed":null,"Judging":null,"Points":null,"Notes":null},"Markdown":"## About this competition\\n- Junior Bagpipes: P03 Novice (a 2/4 March; for pipers in their first full year of playing), P04 Under 16 (a 2/4 March, at least 4 parts), P05 Under 16 (Strathspey and Reel, at least 4 parts), P06 Under 16 (a Jig, at least 4 parts), P07 Under 16 (the ground of a Piobaireachd), P08 Under 16 (Piobaireachd), P09 16-18 (a 2/4 March, Strathspey and Reel, at least 4 parts each), P10 16-18 (a Hornpipe and Jig), P11 16-18 (Piobaireachd). All own choice.\\n- Senior Bagpipes: P101 Premier and A Grade players, Ceol Mor (Piobaireachd, competitor's choice); P102 Premier and A Grade players, Ceol Beag (a March, Strathspey and Reel); P103 B Grade players, Ceol Mor (Piobaireachd, competitor's choice); P104 B Grade players, Ceol Beag (a March, Strathspey and Reel).\\n\\n## Rules\\n- Junior piping is open to players aged 18 and under who have not competed in any adult Bagpipe competition.\\n- Competitors must be in the age group on {{AgeDate}}.\\n- Competitors in P03 cannot enter other events except P07, subject to age.\\n- Competitors entering P08 cannot also compete in P07.\\n- Competitors who have played a full Piobaireachd in any competition cannot compete in P07.\\n- Competitors may only enter events in their own age group."},{"Title":"Chanter","Source":"Rules (English, Jan 2026) Piping; Junior Syllabus p.6","Sections":{"About":["Junior Chanter, Under 13, own choice: P01 (a 2/4 March, 2 parts only) and P02 (a Gaelic Air, 2 parts only)."],"TimeAndFormat":null,"Rules":["Competitors in P01 and P02 cannot enter other events.","Junior piping is open to players aged 18 and under who have not competed in any adult Bagpipe competition.","Competitors must be in the age group on {{AgeDate}}."],"Allowed":null,"NotAllowed":null,"Judging":null,"Points":null,"Notes":null},"Markdown":"## About this competition\\n- Junior Chanter, Under 13, own choice: P01 (a 2/4 March, 2 parts only) and P02 (a Gaelic Air, 2 parts only).\\n\\n## Rules\\n- Competitors in P01 and P02 cannot enter other events.\\n- Junior piping is open to players aged 18 and under who have not competed in any adult Bagpipe competition.\\n- Competitors must be in the age group on {{AgeDate}}."},{"Title":"Fiddle","Source":"Rules (English, Jan 2026) Instrumental; Junior Syllabus p.5; Senior Syllabus p.4","Sections":{"About":["Junior: I08 Under 13 (own choice in the Highland, Gaelic or piping idiom, maximum 5 minutes), I09 SA Under 19 (a slow Air), I09 MSR Under 19 (a March, Strathspey and Reel), I10 Under 19 Fiddle Group (a minimum of 3 tunes in different tempos in traditional Highland style).","Senior: I100 Fiddle (a slow Gaelic Air and March, Strathspey and Reel, competitor's choice), I101 Fiddle Groups (a slow Gaelic Air, March, Strathspey and Reel; Scottish traditional tunes, competitor's choice), I104 Gaelic Waltz Set (accordion or fiddle, with piano accompaniment if wanted, at least 3 waltzes, own choice, maximum 4 minutes)."],"TimeAndFormat":null,"Rules":["Fiddle groups have at least three fiddles and may have up to six other instruments. A group has at most eleven players.","A competitor may not play in more than one group in the same competition.","Competitors may look at their music. Give clear copies of own-choice music to the adjudicator or chairperson on the day."],"Allowed":null,"NotAllowed":null,"Judging":null,"Points":null,"Notes":null},"Markdown":"## About this competition\\n- Junior: I08 Under 13 (own choice in the Highland, Gaelic or piping idiom, maximum 5 minutes), I09 SA Under 19 (a slow Air), I09 MSR Under 19 (a March, Strathspey and Reel), I10 Under 19 Fiddle Group (a minimum of 3 tunes in different tempos in traditional Highland style).\\n- Senior: I100 Fiddle (a slow Gaelic Air and March, Strathspey and Reel, competitor's choice), I101 Fiddle Groups (a slow Gaelic Air, March, Strathspey and Reel; Scottish traditional tunes, competitor's choice), I104 Gaelic Waltz Set (accordion or fiddle, with piano accompaniment if wanted, at least 3 waltzes, own choice, maximum 4 minutes).\\n\\n## Rules\\n- Fiddle groups have at least three fiddles and may have up to six other instruments. A group has at most eleven players.\\n- A competitor may not play in more than one group in the same competition.\\n- Competitors may look at their music. Give clear copies of own-choice music to the adjudicator or chairperson on the day."},{"Title":"Accordion","Source":"Rules (English, Jan 2026) Instrumental; Junior Syllabus p.5; Senior Syllabus p.4","Sections":{"About":["Piano or 3/4/5 row button accordion with Stradella bass. Junior: I03 Under 13 (own choice in the Highland, Gaelic or piping idiom, maximum 5 minutes), I04 Under 19 (a March, Strathspey and Reel), I05 Under 19 (a selection of contemporary traditional tunes, own choice). Senior: I102 Accordion (a Highland March, Strathspey and Reel in traditional style)."],"TimeAndFormat":null,"Rules":["Competitors may look at their music. Give clear copies of own-choice music to the adjudicator or chairperson on the day."],"Allowed":null,"NotAllowed":null,"Judging":null,"Points":null,"Notes":null},"Markdown":"## About this competition\\n- Piano or 3/4/5 row button accordion with Stradella bass. Junior: I03 Under 13 (own choice in the Highland, Gaelic or piping idiom, maximum 5 minutes), I04 Under 19 (a March, Strathspey and Reel), I05 Under 19 (a selection of contemporary traditional tunes, own choice). Senior: I102 Accordion (a Highland March, Strathspey and Reel in traditional style).\\n\\n## Rules\\n- Competitors may look at their music. Give clear copies of own-choice music to the adjudicator or chairperson on the day."},{"Title":"Melodeon","Source":"Rules (English, Jan 2026) Instrumental; Junior Syllabus p.5; Senior Syllabus p.4","Sections":{"About":["Single or double row melodeon with simple diatonic bass. Junior: I06 Under 13 (own choice, maximum 5 minutes), I07 Under 19 (a March, Strathspey and Reel). Senior: I103 Melodeon (a selection of traditional tunes, own choice, maximum 5 minutes)."],"TimeAndFormat":null,"Rules":["Competitors may look at their music. Give clear copies of own-choice music to the adjudicator or chairperson on the day."],"Allowed":null,"NotAllowed":null,"Judging":null,"Points":null,"Notes":null},"Markdown":"## About this competition\\n- Single or double row melodeon with simple diatonic bass. Junior: I06 Under 13 (own choice, maximum 5 minutes), I07 Under 19 (a March, Strathspey and Reel). Senior: I103 Melodeon (a selection of traditional tunes, own choice, maximum 5 minutes).\\n\\n## Rules\\n- Competitors may look at their music. Give clear copies of own-choice music to the adjudicator or chairperson on the day."},{"Title":"Clarsach","Source":"Rules (English, Jan 2026) Instrumental; Junior Syllabus p.6-7; Senior Syllabus p.5","Sections":{"About":["Junior Clarsach: CL01 Self-accompaniment (own choice of 2 contrasting Gaelic songs with Clarsach accompaniment, up to 5 minutes), CL02 Introductory (own choice instrumental piece of Gaelic origin, maximum 5 minutes), CL03 Elementary (Grade 1), CL04 Intermediate 1 (Grade 2-3), CL05 Intermediate 2 (Grade 3-4), CL06 Advanced (Grade 5), CL07 Junior Recital (grade 6 and above, a 10-minute programme of own-choice music of Gaelic origin with spoken introductions), CL08 Junior Duets (Under 13, mixed abilities) and CL09 Junior Duets (13-18, mixed abilities), each own choice of a piece or set of pieces of Gaelic origin, up to 5 minutes, plus CL105 Clarsach Composition (Open) and CL106 Clarsach Ensembles (Open) as one competition with the Senior entrants.","In CL03 to CL06, solo playing of 2 pieces of Gaelic origin: one own choice (up to 3 minutes, 4 minutes for CL06) and one prescribed piece.","Senior Clarsach: CL100 Senior Grade 1 (Senior Elementary) and CL102 Senior Grade 2-5 (Senior Intermediate/Advanced), each solo playing of two pieces of Gaelic origin, one own choice (up to 3 minutes) and one prescribed piece (printed as TBC); CL103 Senior Self-Accompaniment (mixed abilities; solo singing of two contrasting songs of own choice with self-accompaniment, up to 5 minutes in total); CL104 Senior Clarsach Duets (mixed abilities; own choice of a piece or set of pieces of Gaelic origin, up to 5 minutes); CL105 Composition (Open) and CL106 Ensembles (Open), one competition for Junior and Senior entrants, mixed abilities.","Composition: an original piece of 3 to 5 minutes, either solo Clarsach in the style of a slow air, pipe march, strathspey, jig, reel or a set of tunes, or voice and Clarsach. Manuscripts are not required. Ensembles: any number of instruments or voices with at least three Clarsachs, own choice of Gaelic origin, up to 6 minutes."],"TimeAndFormat":null,"Rules":["Own-choice music should be of Scottish Gaelic origin: the melody of a Scottish Gaelic song, or a dance tune or slow air from the Scottish Gaelic tradition, in the Scottish Gaelic idiom.","Grades follow the RCS Scottish Traditional Music Exam syllabus. Own-choice pieces should be of a similar standard and given to the adjudicator on the day.","Questions about Clarsach competitions go to {{ClarsachContact}}.","Competitors may look at their music. Give clear copies of own-choice music to the adjudicator or chairperson on the day."],"Allowed":null,"NotAllowed":null,"Judging":null,"Points":null,"Notes":null},"Markdown":"## About this competition\\n- Junior Clarsach: CL01 Self-accompaniment (own choice of 2 contrasting Gaelic songs with Clarsach accompaniment, up to 5 minutes), CL02 Introductory (own choice instrumental piece of Gaelic origin, maximum 5 minutes), CL03 Elementary (Grade 1), CL04 Intermediate 1 (Grade 2-3), CL05 Intermediate 2 (Grade 3-4), CL06 Advanced (Grade 5), CL07 Junior Recital (grade 6 and above, a 10-minute programme of own-choice music of Gaelic origin with spoken introductions), CL08 Junior Duets (Under 13, mixed abilities) and CL09 Junior Duets (13-18, mixed abilities), each own choice of a piece or set of pieces of Gaelic origin, up to 5 minutes, plus CL105 Clarsach Composition (Open) and CL106 Clarsach Ensembles (Open) as one competition with the Senior entrants.\\n- In CL03 to CL06, solo playing of 2 pieces of Gaelic origin: one own choice (up to 3 minutes, 4 minutes for CL06) and one prescribed piece.\\n- Senior Clarsach: CL100 Senior Grade 1 (Senior Elementary) and CL102 Senior Grade 2-5 (Senior Intermediate/Advanced), each solo playing of two pieces of Gaelic origin, one own choice (up to 3 minutes) and one prescribed piece (printed as TBC); CL103 Senior Self-Accompaniment (mixed abilities; solo singing of two contrasting songs of own choice with self-accompaniment, up to 5 minutes in total); CL104 Senior Clarsach Duets (mixed abilities; own choice of a piece or set of pieces of Gaelic origin, up to 5 minutes); CL105 Composition (Open) and CL106 Ensembles (Open), one competition for Junior and Senior entrants, mixed abilities.\\n- Composition: an original piece of 3 to 5 minutes, either solo Clarsach in the style of a slow air, pipe march, strathspey, jig, reel or a set of tunes, or voice and Clarsach. Manuscripts are not required. Ensembles: any number of instruments or voices with at least three Clarsachs, own choice of Gaelic origin, up to 6 minutes.\\n\\n## Rules\\n- Own-choice music should be of Scottish Gaelic origin: the melody of a Scottish Gaelic song, or a dance tune or slow air from the Scottish Gaelic tradition, in the Scottish Gaelic idiom.\\n- Grades follow the RCS Scottish Traditional Music Exam syllabus. Own-choice pieces should be of a similar standard and given to the adjudicator on the day.\\n- Questions about Clarsach competitions go to {{ClarsachContact}}.\\n- Competitors may look at their music. Give clear copies of own-choice music to the adjudicator or chairperson on the day."},{"Title":"Highland Dancing","Source":"Junior Syllabus p.8","Sections":{"About":["Junior Highland Dancing. Dances: A 16 Pas de Basques, B Pas de Basque and High Cuts, C Highland Fling, D Sword Dance, E Seann Truibhas, F Hullachan (Half Tulloch), G Trophy Highland Fling, H Flora McDonald, I Scottish Lilt, J Barrack's Johnny, K Sailors' Hornpipe.","Primary (4, 5 and 6 years): dances A B C D. Beginner and Novice (8 years and under; 9 and under 11; 11 and under 13; 13 and under 16): C D E F G H I. Intermediate (9 and under; 10 and under 12; 12 and under 14; 14 and under 17): C D E F G H I K. Premier (under 10; 10, 11, 12 and 13; 14, 15 and 16 and over): C D E F G H I K."],"TimeAndFormat":null,"Rules":["All competitors must be registered with the SOBHD and show all registration cards.","The organisers may change age groups according to the entries.","Annual trophies are given in each age group over all dances, except the Primary section.","Entries may be made at {{HighlandDancingEntry}}."],"Allowed":null,"NotAllowed":null,"Judging":null,"Points":null,"Notes":null},"Markdown":"## About this competition\\n- Junior Highland Dancing. Dances: A 16 Pas de Basques, B Pas de Basque and High Cuts, C Highland Fling, D Sword Dance, E Seann Truibhas, F Hullachan (Half Tulloch), G Trophy Highland Fling, H Flora McDonald, I Scottish Lilt, J Barrack's Johnny, K Sailors' Hornpipe.\\n- Primary (4, 5 and 6 years): dances A B C D. Beginner and Novice (8 years and under; 9 and under 11; 11 and under 13; 13 and under 16): C D E F G H I. Intermediate (9 and under; 10 and under 12; 12 and under 14; 14 and under 17): C D E F G H I K. Premier (under 10; 10, 11, 12 and 13; 14, 15 and 16 and over): C D E F G H I K.\\n\\n## Rules\\n- All competitors must be registered with the SOBHD and show all registration cards.\\n- The organisers may change age groups according to the entries.\\n- Annual trophies are given in each age group over all dances, except the Primary section.\\n- Entries may be made at {{HighlandDancingEntry}}."},{"Title":"Cogadh nan Comhlan (Battle of the Bands)","Source":"Rules (English, Jan 2026) Cogadh nan Comhlan; Junior Syllabus p.5","Sections":{"About":["I11 Under 13 heat and I13 Under 13 final; I12 Under 19 heat and I14 Under 19 final. The Under 13 heat is on the Monday of Mod week and the Under 19 heat on the Tuesday, each with its final the same evening."],"TimeAndFormat":null,"Rules":["Bands have at least 3 and at most 8 members for any one set.","In the first heat, bands play one traditional instrumental set that includes Gaelic song. If the band reaches the final, it plays two sets or pieces that can be instrumental pieces including Gaelic song. Bands can play their qualifying piece in the final.","A PA system is provided. Send the band's technical specification with the online entry. For special PA requests, contact the Mod office well in advance.","A competitor may not sing or play in more than one group in the same competition.","Bands (members of bands) may only win this competition once.","Band members may not look at words or music during the performance.","An Comunn Gaidhealach tells entrants their sound check time. Bands must keep to their times."],"Allowed":null,"NotAllowed":null,"Judging":null,"Points":null,"Notes":null},"Markdown":"## About this competition\\n- I11 Under 13 heat and I13 Under 13 final; I12 Under 19 heat and I14 Under 19 final. The Under 13 heat is on the Monday of Mod week and the Under 19 heat on the Tuesday, each with its final the same evening.\\n\\n## Rules\\n- Bands have at least 3 and at most 8 members for any one set.\\n- In the first heat, bands play one traditional instrumental set that includes Gaelic song. If the band reaches the final, it plays two sets or pieces that can be instrumental pieces including Gaelic song. Bands can play their qualifying piece in the final.\\n- A PA system is provided. Send the band's technical specification with the online entry. For special PA requests, contact the Mod office well in advance.\\n- A competitor may not sing or play in more than one group in the same competition.\\n- Bands (members of bands) may only win this competition once.\\n- Band members may not look at words or music during the performance.\\n- An Comunn Gaidhealach tells entrants their sound check time. Bands must keep to their times."},{"Title":"Art (Media, Art and Literature)","Source":"Junior Syllabus p.7-8; Rules (English, Jan 2026) Media, Art and Literature","Sections":{"About":["Junior Art - Open, own choice: E01 5-6 Years, E02 7-8 Years, E03 9-10 Years, E04 11-12 Years, E05 13-15 Years. Make a poster with Gaelic words on a topic of your own choice or on {{MediaTheme}}. Only your pen name goes on the poster.","Related Junior classes in the same section: Literacy - Open L01 9-10, L02 11-12, L03 13-15, L04 16-18 (a short story, poem or newspaper article on a topic of your choice or on {{MediaTheme}}, with the pen-name at the top of the first sheet); M01 Radio Piece / Podcast (up to 10 minutes on {{MediaTheme}}, an MP3 file, up to 6 pupils); M02 Short Film / Vlog (up to 10 minutes on {{MediaTheme}}); TikTok (a Gaelic TikTok video up to 3 minutes that promotes Gaelic language and culture; comedy videos are welcome; entrants at least 13 years old; upload by {{TikTokDeadline}} tagging the Royal National Mod with {{TikTokHashtag}})."],"TimeAndFormat":null,"Rules":["Art: send all work as a PDF or JPEG by {{EntryDeadline}}.","Use a pen-name; only the pen-name may appear on the work."],"Allowed":null,"NotAllowed":null,"Judging":null,"Points":null,"Notes":null},"Markdown":"## About this competition\\n- Junior Art - Open, own choice: E01 5-6 Years, E02 7-8 Years, E03 9-10 Years, E04 11-12 Years, E05 13-15 Years. Make a poster with Gaelic words on a topic of your own choice or on {{MediaTheme}}. Only your pen name goes on the poster.\\n- Related Junior classes in the same section: Literacy - Open L01 9-10, L02 11-12, L03 13-15, L04 16-18 (a short story, poem or newspaper article on a topic of your choice or on {{MediaTheme}}, with the pen-name at the top of the first sheet); M01 Radio Piece / Podcast (up to 10 minutes on {{MediaTheme}}, an MP3 file, up to 6 pupils); M02 Short Film / Vlog (up to 10 minutes on {{MediaTheme}}); TikTok (a Gaelic TikTok video up to 3 minutes that promotes Gaelic language and culture; comedy videos are welcome; entrants at least 13 years old; upload by {{TikTokDeadline}} tagging the Royal National Mod with {{TikTokHashtag}}).\\n\\n## Rules\\n- Art: send all work as a PDF or JPEG by {{EntryDeadline}}.\\n- Use a pen-name; only the pen-name may appear on the work."}]`),r=[],s=[{Key:"ModDates",Kind:"date",Label:"Dates of the Royal National Mod",Value:"2026-10-09 to 2026-10-17",Source:"Entry site banner (9 - 17 An Damhair / October 2026)"},{Key:"Venue",Kind:"address",Label:"Host city",Value:{Name:"The Royal National Mod Glasgow 2026 (Mod Ghlaschu)",Address:"",City:"Glasgow",MapUrl:"",Directions:""},Source:"Entry site banner; the documents print no individual venue"},{Key:"EntryDeadline",Kind:"date",Label:"Entries complete and paid by",Value:"2026-05-31",Source:"Rules (English, Jan 2026) General rule 2 (23:59 on 31st May 2026)"},{Key:"EntryDeadlineTime",Kind:"time",Label:"Entry deadline time",Value:"23:59",Source:"Rules (English, Jan 2026) General rule 2"},{Key:"OwnChoiceDeadline",Kind:"date",Label:"Own-choice music and supporting material submitted by",Value:"2026-05-31",Source:"Rules (English, Jan 2026) General rule 3 and Own Choice Music"},{Key:"MinorChangesDeadline",Kind:"date",Label:"Last date for minor changes to own-choice pieces",Value:"2026-08-31",Source:"Rules (English, Jan 2026) Own Choice Music (not accepted after 31st August 2026)"},{Key:"GaelicAssessmentDeadline",Kind:"date",Label:"Gaelic Assessment completed by",Value:"2026-05-31",Source:"Rules (English, Jan 2026) Gaelic Assessment rule 89"},{Key:"AgeDate",Kind:"date",Label:"Date on which junior age groups are fixed",Value:"2026-10-01",Source:"Rules (English, Jan 2026) Fluency/Age; Guidelines for Families, Schools and Tutors; Chanter/Bagpipes rule 2 (first day of October of the year of the Mod)"},{Key:"TikTokDeadline",Kind:"date",Label:"Last date to upload a TikTok video",Value:"2026-10-18",Source:"Junior Syllabus p.8; Senior Syllabus p.4 (Sunday 18th October 2026)"},{Key:"TikTokHashtag",Kind:"text",Label:"Hashtag for TikTok videos",Value:"#modglaschu2026 (printed #modghlaschu2026 in the Senior Syllabus)",Source:"Junior Syllabus p.8; Senior Syllabus p.4"},{Key:"TrophyReturnDeadline",Kind:"date",Label:"Trophies returned by",Value:"31 July of the following year",Source:"Rules (English, Jan 2026) Trophies"},{Key:"TrophyLateFee",Kind:"fee",Label:"Late return of a trophy",Value:"GBP 20 for each 30-day overdue period (or part of it)",Source:"Rules (English, Jan 2026) Trophies"},{Key:"ProgrammeFee",Kind:"fee",Label:"Printed Competition Programme (posted to a UK address)",Value:"GBP 6.00 for members, GBP 8.00 for non-members. Not posted after 1 October 2026 00:00 GMT.",Source:"Entry site Products page"},{Key:"EntryFees",Kind:"fee",Label:"Entry fees",Value:"Shown by the online entry system when you enter; the syllabus and rules print no entry fee",Source:"Rules (English, Jan 2026) General rule 2 (entries must be paid for); no amount is printed"},{Key:"EntrySystem",Kind:"link",Label:"Online entry system",Value:"https://enter.ancomunn.co.uk",Source:"Rules (English, Jan 2026) General rule 2"},{Key:"HeadOfficeContact",Kind:"contact",Label:"Head office (online entry system enquiries)",Value:{Name:"An Comunn Gaidhealach, Stornoway (Head Office)",Role:"Entry system enquiries",Phone:"01851 703487",WhatsApp:"",Email:"info@ancomunn.co.uk"},Source:"Entry site Contact page"},{Key:"TrophyOffice",Kind:"address",Label:"Office to which trophies are returned",Value:{Name:"An Comunn Gaidhealach office",Address:"5 Mitchell's Lane",City:"Inverness, IV2 3HQ",MapUrl:"",Directions:""},Source:"Rules (English, Jan 2026) Trophies"},{Key:"ClarsachContact",Kind:"contact",Label:"Comunn nan Clarsach administrator (Clarsach questions)",Value:{Name:"Comunn nan Clarsach administrator",Role:"Clarsach competitions",Phone:"",WhatsApp:"",Email:"gaelic@clarsachsociety.co.uk"},Source:"Rules (English, Jan 2026) Instrumental competitions"},{Key:"PipingContact",Kind:"contact",Label:"Competing Pipers Association (Senior Piping)",Value:{Name:"The Competing Pipers Association",Role:"Senior Piping competitions",Phone:"",WhatsApp:"",Email:"cpa_sec@yahoo.co.uk"},Source:"Rules (English, Jan 2026) Piping; Senior Syllabus p.4"},{Key:"HighlandDancingEntry",Kind:"link",Label:"Highland Dancing entry page",Value:"www.toeandheel.com",Source:"Junior Syllabus p.8"},{Key:"SeniorSolosTheme",Kind:"text",Label:"Theme for the Learners Silver Pendant own-choice songs",Value:"Eileanan / Islands",Source:"Senior Syllabus p.1 (S112, S113)"},{Key:"OpenRecitationTheme",Kind:"text",Label:"Theme for the Senior Open Recitation (BA100, BA101)",Value:"Sith / Peace",Source:"Senior Syllabus p.3"},{Key:"MediaTheme",Kind:"text",Label:"Theme for Junior Art, Literacy, Radio and Film",Value:"Coimhearsnachd / Community",Source:"Junior Syllabus p.7-8"},{Key:"Announcements",Kind:"news",Label:"News printed with the syllabus",Value:[{Date:"2026-05-11",Title:"Prescribed pieces amendments (version 2)",Text:"Prescribed Pieces Amendments were uploaded on 11/05/26. They correct words, instructions and bar numbering in these prescribed pieces: S04 Junior Fluent Primary 4, S11 Junior Learners P2 and 3, S15 Junior Learners 13-15, C01, C03, C04 and C13 (Junior Choral), S114 (M) Silver Pendant Final, S103 Gold Medal Qualifier Men, C102, C103, C106, C107 and C108 (Senior Choral). Download the file and use the corrected version.",Url:"https://enter.ancomunn.co.uk/file/pdf/pdf/Prescribed_Changes_acgmod_event_18"},{Date:"",Title:"Adjudication sheets are now online",Text:"Adjudication sheets are uploaded to the competitor's online entry account. They are no longer available to collect during Mod week.",Url:""},{Date:"",Title:"Virtual Mod for those who cannot attend",Text:"Virtual competitions are for entrants who cannot or do not wish to attend in Glasgow. They send a pre-recorded video and get feedback from adjudicators. No first, second or third place is given: entrants get Commended, Highly Commended or Exceptional.",Url:"https://enter.ancomunn.co.uk/file/pdf/pdf/Syllabus_Virtual_acgmod_event_18"}],Source:"Prescribed_Changes (3 pages); Rules Adjudication; Virtual Syllabus"}],l=[{Claim:"The rules, deadlines, eligibility, syllabus classes, guidelines and amendments are those of The Royal National Mod Glasgow 2026 documents on the organiser's entry site.",SourceType:"Primary - organiser's own rules and syllabi",Source:"An Comunn Gaidhealach, The Royal National Mod Glasgow 2026: Rules (English), Junior and Senior and Virtual Syllabus, Guidelines, Prescribed Pieces Amendments",Url:"https://enter.ancomunn.co.uk/pages/syllabus"},{Claim:"The documents print no entry fees, marking scheme, points or venues, so those parts are empty or held as Variables with the printed words. Nothing was written from general knowledge.",SourceType:"Primary - organiser's own rules and syllabi",Source:"An Comunn Gaidhealach, The Royal National Mod Glasgow 2026 documents",Url:"https://enter.ancomunn.co.uk/pages/syllabus"}],c={TemplateKey:e,Year:2026,Note:n,Page:o,ParticipantTypes:i,AgeGroups:a,Competitions:t,CompetitionAgeGroups:r,Variables:s,Evidence:l};export{a as AgeGroups,r as CompetitionAgeGroups,t as Competitions,l as Evidence,n as Note,o as Page,i as ParticipantTypes,e as TemplateKey,s as Variables,d as Year,c as default};
